###### Abstract

W\({}_{x}\)NbMoTa refractory high-entropy alloys with four different tungsten concentrations (\(x=0,0.16,0.33,0.53\)) were fabricated by laser cladding deposition. The crystal structures of W\({}_{x}\)NbMoTa alloys are all a single-phase solid solution of the body-centered cubic (BCC) structure. The size of the grains and dendrites are 20 \(\upmu\)m and 4 \(\upmu\)m on average, due to the rapid solidification characteristics of the laser cladding deposition. These are much smaller sizes than refractory high-entropy alloys fabricated by vacuum arc melting. In terms of integrated mechanical properties, the increase of the tungsten concentration of W\({}_{x}\)NbMoTa has led to four results of the Vickers microhardness, i.e., \(H_{0}=459.2\pm 9.7\), \(476.0\pm 12.9\), \(485.3\pm 8.7\), and \(497.6\pm 5.6\). As a result, NbMoTa alloy shows a yield strength (\(\sigma_{\text{b}}\)) and compressive strain (\(\epsilon_{\text{p}}\)) of 530 Mpa and 8.5% at 1000 \({}^{\circ}\)C, leading to better results than traditional refractory alloys such as T-111, C103, and Nb-1Zr, which are commonly used in the aerospace industry.

W\({}_{x}\)NbMoTa; refractory high-entropy alloy; laser cladding deposition; rapid solidification +
